WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:10.380
Today we are not going to marvel at neural&nbsp;
network-based AI systems, but we are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:18.051
to defeat them. What? How? Well, of course,&nbsp;
not with brute force, but with trickery.

00:00:18.051 --> 00:00:24.720
You see, this is what we call an adversarial&nbsp;
attack. And few know about it, but many modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.060
AI techniques are quite vulnerable against&nbsp;
them. This is the “You Shall Not Pass” game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:35.040
where the red agent is trying to hold&nbsp;
back the blue character and not let it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:41.580
cross the line. Here you see two regular&nbsp;
AIs duking it out, sometimes the red wins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:48.060
sometimes the blue is able to get through. Nothing&nbsp;
too crazy here. This is the reference case which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:55.860
is somewhat well balanced. Now, look closely,&nbsp;
because here comes the hacker adversarial agent.&nbsp;

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:04.860
Ha! Yes, you are seeing correctly, this chap&nbsp;
it doing nothing. Absolutely nothing. But it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:10.560
is doing nothing in a way that reprograms&nbsp;
its opponent to make mistakes and behave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:16.620
close to a completely randomly acting&nbsp;
agent! This paper was absolute insanity.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:23.340
A different adversarial attack paper showed an&nbsp;
interesting case where we were able to take an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:29.940
image of a horse. The attacked neural network&nbsp;
indeed recognized that this was a horse. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:38.520
when changing just one pixel, an otherwise quite&nbsp;
competent AI now thinks that this is a frog. Note&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:45.000
that this does not mean that we can just change&nbsp;
any pixel anywhere. This is a sophisticated attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.140
that knows who it is attacking and how this&nbsp;
one pixel difference will reprogram its brain.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:58.200
Similar attacks also exist that do this&nbsp;
with a little more flavor. Look. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:04.800
is a bus. And this is noise. And when we&nbsp;
add this together, do we get a bus+noise?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:10.620
Nope, what we get is an&nbsp;
ostrich. So does the AI think.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:14.700
Now, let’s have a look at&nbsp;
new papers with new attacks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:19.440
first against AIs that can&nbsp;
play Go. Now, wait a second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:25.200
what is really new here? We have heard&nbsp;
players reporting before that with AlphaZero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:32.040
they have experienced little hiccups where the&nbsp;
AI made suboptimal moves in an otherwise really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:37.860
well played game. Why is that news? Why write&nbsp;
a paper about this? How is this one different?

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:43.140
What makes this attack interesting&nbsp;
is that this is not a one-off fluke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:50.700
this is an AI that finds systematic flaws in&nbsp;
other neural network-based systems. This means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:55.620
that they are not only able to exploit&nbsp;
their weaknesses and win against them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:02.280
but they can do it on a consistent basis. In&nbsp;
other words, they can win over and over again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:08.280
In this case, hold on to your papers, because&nbsp;
this paper describes an attack that is able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:17.700
defeat KataGo in 97% of the games. That number is&nbsp;
unreal. Let me explain why. From what I have seen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:25.680
KataGo seems to be even stronger than AlphaZero&nbsp;
and AlphaGo Zero, DeepMind’s legendary systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:31.500
that are able to beat the best human players&nbsp;
in the world. They authors note that it works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:36.840
on many AlphaGo-based systems, and it&nbsp;
likely works on AlphaGo variants too.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:42.960
What makes it even more impressive is that&nbsp;
this adversary was trained from scratch and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.520
did not use any human knowledge.&nbsp;
It found this out all by itself.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:55.440
And finally, here is another attack against&nbsp;
a competent image recognition AI. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.940
showing it the Starry Night painting&nbsp;
by van Gogh, which it will, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:06.960
recognize as shown the by red frame. However, as&nbsp;
we start adding carefully crafted noise to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.940
nothing happens. But wait just a little&nbsp;
more, as we continue the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:19.380
bam! Now this noise looks nothing like Starry&nbsp;
Night, but not according to this AI. It would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:25.560
swear that this is exactly the Starry Night&nbsp;
painting. And this works for other examples too.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:30.840
The goal of all this is to show you some&nbsp;
of the weaknesses of recent AI systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:35.760
This is a really interesting new field&nbsp;
where we have these powerful new tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.720
but this also means that they&nbsp;
have their own limitations too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:45.540
and sometimes, it is not at all obvious&nbsp;
what they are. More research is required.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:50.760
Now, one word about the LK99, the&nbsp;
room-temperature superconductor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:55.500
project. I see that many of you Fellow&nbsp;
Scholars would love an episode on it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:01.320
however, I do not have the required knowledge&nbsp;
to comment on it. Obviously it would be a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:06.060
good thing for views, but that doesn’t&nbsp;
matter. What matters is that you Fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:10.500
Scholars get the quality of videos that&nbsp;
you expect here. That is what matters.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:59.880
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

